<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Palmyrene Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">260 CE – 273 CE · capital Palmyra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The breakaway realm of the caravan city of Palmyra, which under Queen Zenobia briefly ruled Rome's East from Egypt to Anatolia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> captured the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a> in 260, the Palmyrene noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a> defeated the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and crushed the usurpers in the East, earning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> the title corrector of the whole East. His murder in 267 left power to his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, regent for their young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> had grown rich on caravan trade linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and its elite blended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, Arab, Greek and Roman culture, worshipping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bel-god">Bel</a> and other Semitic gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> kept a learned court that included the rhetorician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-longinus">Longinus</a>. In 270 her armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and in 272 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> was proclaimed Augustus, an open break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The emperor Aurelian defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia's</a> armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-immae">Immae</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emesene-dynasty">Emesa</a> in 272, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and captured her as she fled towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. When the city rebelled again in 273 it was sacked and never recovered its wealth. Zenobia was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palmyrene-empire.jpg" alt="Palmyrene Empire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_of_Palmyra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>260 CE – 273 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palmyra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Odaenathus, Zenobia, Vaballathus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Semitic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Palmyrene Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria, Turkey, Egypt, Lebanon, Israel, Jordan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Palmyra, Kingdom of Palmyra</dd></dl></aside></div>
